# 1. Introduction

## 1.1 Purpose

The purpose of this Software Requirements Specification (SRS) document is to define the complete functional and non-functional requirements for the Distributed Web-Based Chat Application. The document serves as a formal agreement between developers, stakeholders, project supervisors, testers, and future maintainers by describing every aspect of the system before development begins. It provides a clear understanding of the application's objectives, architecture, constraints, expected behavior, interfaces, and quality requirements. This specification acts as the primary reference throughout the software development life cycle, ensuring consistency during design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance.

The proposed application is designed as a scalable, real-time communication platform that enables users to exchange text messages, multimedia files, and notifications instantly across multiple distributed servers. Unlike traditional chat applications that rely on a single centralized server, the proposed system adopts distributed computing principles to ensure high availability, fault tolerance, improved scalability, and better performance under heavy user loads. The architecture demonstrates practical implementation of distributed systems concepts such as horizontal scaling, message synchronization using publish-subscribe mechanisms, load balancing, stateless server design, and distributed caching.

---

## 1.2 Scope

The Distributed Web-Based Chat Application is intended to provide secure and reliable real-time communication over the Internet using modern web technologies. Users will be able to register, authenticate themselves securely, create personal profiles, search for other users, initiate private conversations, create group chats, exchange files, react to messages, receive notifications, and monitor user presence in real time.

The backend system will be implemented using multiple independent chat servers communicating through Redis Pub/Sub. Messages generated on one server instance will be synchronized with all other active server instances, allowing users connected to different servers to communicate seamlessly without experiencing inconsistencies.

The application will also implement secure authentication using JSON Web Tokens (JWT), encrypted password storage using bcrypt, persistent message storage using MongoDB, and scalable deployment through Docker containers. Nginx will function as the reverse proxy and load balancer to distribute incoming traffic across multiple backend instances.

The project primarily aims to demonstrate distributed systems concepts rather than simply building another messaging platform. Therefore, significant emphasis is placed on scalability, fault tolerance, concurrent user handling, synchronization, observability, and maintainability.

---

## 1.3 Objectives

The major objective of the project is to design and implement a highly scalable distributed messaging platform capable of supporting thousands of concurrent users while maintaining low latency and high availability.

Specific objectives include designing a responsive web application that supports instant messaging through WebSockets, implementing distributed communication using Redis Pub/Sub, enabling horizontal scaling through multiple backend instances, developing secure authentication and authorization mechanisms, providing persistent message storage, ensuring system resilience against server failures, and maintaining synchronization across distributed nodes.

Another objective is to expose students to real-world distributed architecture involving multiple independent services instead of monolithic application development. The project also aims to familiarize developers with Docker containerization, reverse proxy configuration, distributed caching, monitoring, logging, and deployment automation.

