# 2. Overall Description

## 2.1 Product Perspective

The proposed system is a web-based distributed messaging platform inspired by modern applications such as Slack, Discord, Microsoft Teams, and WhatsApp Web. However, unlike these commercial systems, the application is specifically designed as an educational implementation of distributed computing concepts while remaining production-oriented.

The architecture follows a layered client-server model with distributed backend services. The frontend interacts with backend REST APIs for authentication and data retrieval while maintaining persistent WebSocket connections for real-time communication. Incoming requests first reach the Nginx reverse proxy, which distributes traffic among multiple chat server instances. Each server maintains active socket connections while synchronizing all events through Redis Pub/Sub. MongoDB stores user information, conversations, and message history permanently.

This architecture ensures that even if users are connected to different backend servers, messages remain synchronized across the entire distributed network.

---

## 2.2 Product Functions

The system provides comprehensive communication functionality suitable for modern messaging applications.

Users can register new accounts, securely log in, manage personal profiles, search for other registered users, initiate one-to-one conversations, create group chats, invite members, assign administrative privileges, and exchange multimedia files including images, videos, audio files, PDF documents, and compressed archives.

Real-time communication includes instant message delivery, typing indicators, online/offline presence detection, message editing, message deletion, emoji reactions, read receipts, delivery confirmations, pinned messages, threaded replies, forwarded messages, and notification delivery.

Administrative functionality includes user management, moderation tools, role assignment, monitoring server health, viewing system logs, and observing application metrics through integrated dashboards.

---

## 2.3 User Classes

The application consists of multiple user categories with different permissions and responsibilities.

The primary user class consists of ordinary users who register accounts and utilize messaging functionality. These users can participate in private conversations and groups while managing their personal information.

Group administrators possess additional privileges including adding or removing members, assigning moderators, changing group settings, and controlling permissions within their communities.

System administrators manage infrastructure, monitor system performance, inspect logs, configure servers, observe distributed communication, and ensure service availability.

Developers and maintainers utilize administrative dashboards, monitoring tools, and deployment pipelines to update software versions, analyze application metrics, troubleshoot issues, and maintain distributed infrastructure.

---

## 2.4 Operating Environment

The frontend application operates within modern web browsers including Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge, Brave Browser, Safari, and Opera. The interface is fully responsive and optimized for desktop, tablet, and mobile devices.

Backend services execute using Node.js running inside Docker containers on Linux-based operating systems such as Ubuntu Server. Multiple containerized services communicate through Docker Compose networking during development, while Kubernetes may orchestrate deployments in production environments.

MongoDB serves as the persistent NoSQL database. Redis functions as the in-memory publish-subscribe broker and caching layer. Nginx operates as the reverse proxy responsible for SSL termination, request routing, and load balancing.

Monitoring infrastructure includes Prometheus for metrics collection, Grafana for visualization dashboards, Loki for centralized logging, and OpenTelemetry for distributed tracing.

---

## 2.5 Design Constraints

Several constraints influence the implementation of the system.

The project is intended to be developed entirely using free and open-source technologies. No paid cloud services or proprietary software will be required during development.

Backend implementation must remain stateless to enable horizontal scalability. Session information cannot be stored locally within server memory because users may connect to different backend instances during subsequent requests.

Communication between servers must occur exclusively through Redis Pub/Sub instead of direct socket communication. This ensures loose coupling and supports future scalability.

Password storage must never contain plaintext credentials. All passwords shall be securely hashed using bcrypt before storage within MongoDB.

Communication between client and server shall occur over HTTPS in production environments to ensure confidentiality and integrity.

---

## 2.6 Assumptions and Dependencies

The system assumes that users possess stable Internet connectivity capable of maintaining persistent WebSocket connections.

The application depends on MongoDB for persistent storage and Redis for distributed synchronization. Failure of either component will reduce application functionality.

Docker Desktop is assumed to be available for local development environments. Deployment environments must support container execution and reverse proxy configuration.

Cloudinary or equivalent object storage services are assumed to store multimedia files rather than local server storage, preventing backend storage limitations.

