# 3. System Architecture

The Distributed Web-Based Chat Application follows a microservices-inspired distributed architecture designed to achieve scalability, reliability, maintainability, and fault tolerance. Rather than relying on a single monolithic server, the application distributes workload across multiple independent backend instances that communicate through Redis Pub/Sub.

Client requests first arrive at the Nginx reverse proxy. Nginx analyzes incoming traffic and distributes connections among multiple backend servers using configurable load balancing algorithms. This prevents individual servers from becoming overloaded while improving overall application responsiveness.

Each backend server independently handles authentication requests, REST API operations, and persistent WebSocket connections. Because backend servers are stateless, any user request may be processed by any available server instance without requiring session migration.

When a connected user sends a message, the receiving server immediately stores the message within MongoDB and publishes the message event to Redis Pub/Sub. Redis instantly broadcasts the event to every backend server participating in the distributed cluster. Servers receiving the published event identify connected recipients and immediately deliver the message through active WebSocket connections.

This publish-subscribe architecture ensures that users connected to different backend servers experience synchronized conversations despite physical separation of server instances.

Persistent data including user accounts, conversations, message history, and metadata remain stored within MongoDB. Redis maintains temporary information such as online users, typing indicators, and active socket mappings.

This architecture supports horizontal scaling by simply launching additional backend server instances without modifying application logic. As user traffic increases, administrators can deploy more servers behind the Nginx load balancer while Redis automatically synchronizes communication between them.

