# Chapter 5: Non-Functional Requirements

## 5.1 Introduction

Non-functional requirements define the quality attributes, operational characteristics, and performance expectations of the Distributed Web-Based Chat Application. While functional requirements describe what the system does, non-functional requirements specify how effectively the system performs those functions. These requirements ensure that the application is secure, reliable, scalable, maintainable, and capable of supporting real-world usage under varying workloads. Since the proposed system is based on distributed computing principles, particular emphasis is placed on high availability, low latency, fault tolerance, efficient resource utilization, and seamless communication between multiple server instances. Compliance with these requirements is essential for ensuring that the application remains responsive and dependable even when the number of concurrent users increases significantly.

---

## 5.2 Performance Requirements

The Distributed Web-Based Chat Application shall provide real-time communication with minimal response latency. Under normal operating conditions, the system shall deliver text messages between connected users within one second, assuming stable network connectivity. Authentication requests, user searches, profile updates, and conversation retrieval operations shall be processed efficiently to provide a smooth user experience. Database queries shall be optimized through indexing and efficient schema design to minimize execution time.

The backend architecture shall support asynchronous processing wherever appropriate to prevent blocking operations from affecting application responsiveness. Operations such as file uploads, image processing, notification generation, and logging shall execute independently through background workers whenever possible. The application shall maintain acceptable response times during periods of increased user activity without significantly degrading overall performance.

Caching mechanisms shall be implemented using Redis to reduce repeated database queries for frequently accessed information such as user sessions, online status, and active conversation metadata. This approach minimizes unnecessary database load while improving application responsiveness.

---

## 5.3 Scalability Requirements

One of the primary objectives of the proposed system is horizontal scalability. The architecture shall support the addition of multiple backend server instances without requiring modifications to application logic or interruption of existing services. As user traffic increases, administrators shall be able to deploy additional chat servers behind the load balancer to distribute computational workload evenly.

Backend services shall remain stateless, ensuring that no user-specific session information is permanently stored within individual server memory. This design allows incoming requests to be processed by any available server instance regardless of previous interactions. Session persistence shall instead be managed through secure authentication tokens and distributed caching mechanisms.

Redis Pub/Sub shall enable communication among all active backend servers, ensuring that messages generated by one server become immediately available to every other server within the distributed environment. This architecture allows the system to support increasing numbers of concurrent users while maintaining message consistency across all server instances.

The database layer shall also support future horizontal expansion through MongoDB replica sets and sharding if application growth exceeds the capacity of a single database instance.

---

## 5.4 Availability Requirements

The application shall be designed to provide continuous service availability with minimal downtime. Multiple backend servers operating simultaneously ensure that the failure of a single server does not interrupt communication for the entire platform. If one server becomes unavailable, the load balancer shall automatically redirect new client connections to healthy server instances without requiring manual intervention.

Database services shall be configured to support automatic recovery mechanisms through replication and backup strategies. Critical application components shall implement health monitoring to detect service failures quickly and notify administrators of abnormal operating conditions.

The application shall gracefully handle temporary service interruptions by automatically attempting to reconnect disconnected users through WebSocket reconnection mechanisms. Users should experience minimal disruption during transient network failures.

---

## 5.5 Reliability Requirements

Reliability is a critical quality attribute for any communication platform. The system shall consistently deliver messages without unintended duplication, corruption, or data loss. Every message shall first be stored within the database before being published to Redis, ensuring persistence even if temporary communication failures occur.

The distributed synchronization mechanism shall guarantee that all active backend servers receive published message events. Duplicate event processing shall be prevented through unique message identifiers and acknowledgment mechanisms. Database operations shall utilize transactional consistency wherever appropriate to maintain accurate conversation history.

Unexpected server failures shall not result in permanent message loss. Since message data is stored persistently within MongoDB, users shall be able to retrieve previous conversations even after infrastructure restarts or temporary service outages.

Extensive logging shall be implemented throughout the application to facilitate troubleshooting and identify potential reliability issues before they affect end users.

---

## 5.6 Security Requirements

Security represents one of the most important aspects of the Distributed Web-Based Chat Application. All communication between clients and backend servers shall occur through encrypted HTTPS connections in production environments. Sensitive user information including passwords shall never be stored in plain text. Passwords shall be encrypted using the bcrypt hashing algorithm combined with cryptographically secure salt values before storage within the database.

Authentication shall rely upon JSON Web Tokens (JWT) to authorize API requests securely. Access tokens shall have limited validity periods, while refresh tokens shall support controlled session renewal. Unauthorized users attempting to access protected resources shall receive appropriate authentication errors.

Input validation shall be performed on every client request to prevent SQL injection, NoSQL injection, cross-site scripting (XSS), command injection, and other common web application attacks. Uploaded files shall be validated according to file type, size limitations, and content restrictions before permanent storage.

Rate limiting shall be implemented for authentication endpoints and other sensitive APIs to reduce the risk of brute-force attacks and denial-of-service attempts. Application logs shall record suspicious activities including repeated failed login attempts, unauthorized access requests, and administrative actions.

Administrative interfaces shall be protected through role-based access control, ensuring that privileged operations remain accessible only to authorized personnel.

---

## 5.7 Maintainability Requirements

The proposed system shall follow modular software engineering principles to simplify future maintenance and feature development. Independent services such as authentication, messaging, notifications, user management, and media handling shall be organized into separate modules with clearly defined responsibilities.

Source code shall follow standardized coding conventions and include meaningful documentation to improve readability. Reusable components, utility functions, middleware, and configuration files shall minimize duplication throughout the codebase.

Version control shall be maintained through Git, enabling collaborative development, change tracking, and rollback capabilities. Continuous Integration pipelines shall automatically execute testing procedures before software deployment to detect defects early within the development lifecycle.

Configuration values including database credentials, API keys, authentication secrets, and environment-specific parameters shall remain externalized through environment configuration files rather than being hardcoded within application source code.

---

## 5.8 Usability Requirements

The user interface shall prioritize simplicity, responsiveness, and intuitive interaction. Users should be able to navigate between conversations, create groups, search contacts, upload files, and configure account settings without requiring technical expertise or extensive training.

The application shall implement responsive web design principles to ensure compatibility across desktop computers, tablets, and smartphones. Interface components shall automatically adapt to different screen sizes while maintaining consistent functionality.

Visual feedback shall accompany all important user actions including successful authentication, message delivery, upload progress, notification updates, and error handling. Error messages shall provide meaningful explanations and suggest corrective actions whenever possible.

Accessibility guidelines shall be considered throughout interface development by maintaining sufficient color contrast, keyboard navigation support, readable typography, and descriptive labels for interactive elements.

---

## 5.9 Portability Requirements

The Distributed Web-Based Chat Application shall remain platform independent and operate consistently across multiple operating systems and web browsers. Since the frontend is browser-based, users shall not be required to install dedicated software. Any modern browser supporting HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript, and WebSocket communication shall be capable of accessing the application.

Backend services shall execute inside Docker containers, ensuring consistent runtime environments across development, testing, and production systems. Containerization minimizes deployment inconsistencies while simplifying migration between cloud providers or physical servers.

The application architecture shall remain independent of vendor-specific technologies wherever practical, allowing future migration to alternative infrastructure providers without significant code modifications.

---

## 5.10 Interoperability Requirements

The application shall expose standardized RESTful APIs for communication between frontend clients and backend services. API responses shall utilize JSON formatting to maximize compatibility with modern web technologies.

Real-time communication shall rely upon WebSocket protocols implemented through Socket.IO, enabling bidirectional communication between browsers and backend services. External integrations such as authentication providers, cloud storage platforms, notification systems, and monitoring services shall communicate using standardized interfaces wherever available.

Future extensions may integrate mobile applications, artificial intelligence services, chatbot frameworks, or enterprise collaboration platforms without requiring major architectural redesign.

---

## 5.11 Fault Tolerance Requirements

Distributed systems must continue operating despite individual component failures. The proposed application shall tolerate backend server failures by distributing client connections across multiple server instances. When one server becomes unavailable, existing users connected to remaining servers shall continue communicating without interruption.

Redis Pub/Sub shall synchronize message events independently of individual server health, while persistent storage within MongoDB ensures that messages remain recoverable following temporary infrastructure failures.

Automatic WebSocket reconnection mechanisms shall restore client connectivity after transient network interruptions. Health monitoring services shall continuously verify server availability and notify administrators whenever critical components become unavailable.

The architecture shall avoid single points of failure wherever possible by separating messaging, storage, synchronization, and routing responsibilities among independent services.

---

## 5.12 Logging and Monitoring Requirements

Comprehensive monitoring capabilities shall be incorporated to observe application behavior and detect operational issues proactively. All significant events including authentication requests, message delivery, database operations, server errors, administrative activities, and distributed synchronization events shall be recorded within centralized log storage.

Prometheus shall periodically collect system metrics including processor utilization, memory consumption, request throughput, response latency, active WebSocket connections, Redis performance, and MongoDB statistics. Grafana dashboards shall visualize these metrics to assist administrators in evaluating overall system health.

Centralized logging through Loki shall simplify debugging by aggregating logs from every distributed server instance into a single searchable repository. Distributed tracing tools may additionally monitor request flow across multiple services to identify performance bottlenecks and communication delays.

---

## 5.13 Backup and Recovery Requirements

Regular database backups shall be performed to prevent permanent data loss resulting from hardware failures, accidental deletion, or software defects. Backup procedures shall include user accounts, conversations, message history, configuration settings, and administrative records.

Recovery procedures shall enable restoration of application data within acceptable recovery time objectives. Backup verification processes shall periodically confirm the integrity and usability of stored backup files.

Cloud-hosted media files shall remain independently recoverable through external storage providers to ensure continued availability following infrastructure restoration.

---

## 5.14 Compliance and Standards

The development of the Distributed Web-Based Chat Application shall follow recognized software engineering practices and documentation standards inspired by IEEE 29148 for Software Requirements Specifications. Secure coding practices shall align with recommendations published by the Open Worldwide Application Security Project (OWASP) to reduce common web application vulnerabilities.

The application shall respect user privacy by collecting only necessary information required for system operation. Authentication credentials, personal information, and communication records shall be protected according to accepted security best practices.

Development shall emphasize maintainability, modularity, documentation quality, and code readability to support long-term evolution of the software system.

