# Chapter 7: Database Design

## 7.1 Introduction

The database is one of the most critical components of the Distributed Web-Based Chat Application because it provides persistent storage for users, conversations, messages, authentication information, groups, notifications, and system metadata. Unlike traditional relational database systems that organize data into multiple interconnected tables, the proposed application utilizes MongoDB, a NoSQL document-oriented database capable of storing flexible JSON-like documents. MongoDB has been selected because of its scalability, schema flexibility, high performance, horizontal scaling capabilities, and compatibility with modern JavaScript-based application development.

Since the application is expected to support thousands of concurrent users communicating through multiple distributed backend servers, the database must provide reliable persistence while efficiently handling frequent read and write operations. Every message exchanged between users must be stored permanently before being distributed through Redis Pub/Sub. This approach guarantees that conversation history remains available even if temporary network failures or server outages occur.

The database design emphasizes scalability, maintainability, and consistency. Each collection has a clearly defined responsibility, reducing redundancy while allowing future expansion without requiring significant structural modifications. Frequently accessed data shall be indexed appropriately to improve search performance and reduce query execution time. The database architecture also supports replication and future sharding to accommodate increasing workloads as the application grows.

---

# 7.2 Database Architecture

The application adopts a centralized persistent storage architecture in which MongoDB serves as the primary source of truth for all application data. Every backend server communicates directly with the database through secure authenticated connections using the official MongoDB driver. Although multiple chat servers may simultaneously process client requests, all persistent information is stored within the same distributed database cluster.

When a user sends a message, the receiving backend server first validates the request and stores the message within MongoDB. Only after successful database insertion does the server publish the corresponding synchronization event through Redis Pub/Sub. This sequence ensures that messages are never distributed before permanent storage, thereby preventing message loss during unexpected failures.

The database maintains user accounts, conversations, messages, groups, notifications, authentication tokens, file metadata, administrative logs, and system configuration. Temporary information such as online users, typing indicators, and active socket connections is intentionally excluded from MongoDB because such data changes frequently and is instead maintained within Redis for improved performance.

Future deployments may configure MongoDB as a replica set to improve availability and data redundancy. As user volume increases, horizontal partitioning through database sharding can further distribute storage and processing load across multiple database servers.

---

# 7.3 Collections Overview

The database consists of several independent collections, each responsible for storing a specific category of information. Separating data into specialized collections improves maintainability, simplifies query optimization, and reduces unnecessary document complexity.

The **Users** collection stores account information including authentication credentials, profile details, privacy settings, and account status. The **Conversations** collection maintains metadata describing private chats and group conversations without directly storing message content. Individual messages are maintained within the **Messages** collection, enabling efficient retrieval, pagination, and indexing of conversation history.

Group information is maintained within the **Groups** collection, while application notifications are stored separately within the **Notifications** collection. Authentication refresh tokens, password reset requests, and email verification records are managed independently to improve security and simplify lifecycle management. Administrative logs record system activities, moderation actions, and security-related events for auditing purposes.

This modular organization allows each collection to evolve independently as application requirements change.

---

# 7.4 Users Collection

The Users collection stores permanent account information for every registered individual using the application. Each document represents one user and contains personal information, authentication credentials, profile configuration, and application preferences.

Every user document shall include a unique identifier generated automatically by MongoDB. Additional fields include the user's full name, username, email address, encrypted password, profile photograph URL, biography, account creation date, verification status, privacy settings, notification preferences, account role, and account activity status.

Passwords shall never be stored in plain text. Before insertion into the database, passwords shall be encrypted using the bcrypt hashing algorithm combined with a secure cryptographic salt. Email addresses and usernames shall remain unique across the entire collection to prevent duplicate account creation.

Profile information may be updated throughout the lifetime of the account. Since MongoDB stores flexible documents, optional fields such as biographies, social links, or profile customization settings may be introduced in future versions without requiring database restructuring.

Indexes shall be created on username and email fields to accelerate authentication, search operations, and profile lookup requests.

---

# 7.5 Conversations Collection

The Conversations collection stores metadata describing communication channels between users. Rather than storing every message directly inside conversation documents, each conversation maintains only descriptive information while message content resides within the Messages collection.

Each conversation document identifies the conversation type, participants, creation timestamp, latest message reference, unread message counts, archived status, and conversation settings. Conversation types include private one-to-one chats and group discussions.

Maintaining conversation metadata separately improves query performance because retrieving recent conversation lists does not require loading complete message histories. Instead, conversation summaries may be generated efficiently using indexed metadata fields combined with references to the latest messages.

Whenever new messages are exchanged, the latest message reference and conversation timestamp shall be updated automatically, ensuring that recent conversations appear correctly within user dashboards.

---

# 7.6 Messages Collection

The Messages collection represents the largest and most frequently accessed component of the database. Every message exchanged through the application is stored as an independent document within this collection.

Each message document includes a unique message identifier, conversation reference, sender identifier, recipient information, message content, message type, creation timestamp, delivery status, read status, edit history, attachment references, and reaction metadata.

Separating messages into independent documents enables efficient pagination for lengthy conversations. Rather than loading thousands of messages simultaneously, the application retrieves conversation history incrementally according to user scrolling behavior.

Message documents support multiple content types including plain text, images, videos, audio recordings, PDF documents, spreadsheets, compressed archives, and future multimedia extensions. Attachment metadata references externally stored files rather than embedding binary content directly within database documents.

Edited messages retain modification timestamps and version history while deleted messages may preserve minimal metadata for auditing purposes depending upon application configuration.

Indexes shall be created on conversation identifiers, sender identifiers, timestamps, and message identifiers to optimize retrieval operations.

---

# 7.7 Groups Collection

The Groups collection maintains information describing collaborative conversations involving multiple users. Every group document contains administrative information, membership records, configuration settings, and descriptive metadata.

Each group stores its unique identifier, group name, description, administrator list, member list, profile image URL, privacy settings, creation timestamp, invitation permissions, notification preferences, and current operational status.

Rather than storing complete user information inside group documents, only references to user identifiers shall be maintained. This approach minimizes redundancy and ensures that profile updates automatically propagate throughout the application.

Administrative privileges shall support multiple roles including Owner, Administrator, Moderator, and Member. These roles determine permissions such as inviting participants, removing members, editing group settings, and deleting conversations.

Future application versions may extend group functionality by introducing channels, categories, scheduled events, polls, and community moderation features without requiring significant modifications to the existing schema.

---

# 7.8 Notifications Collection

The Notifications collection stores alerts generated throughout application usage. Notifications include new message alerts, group invitations, administrative announcements, reactions, replies, mentions, account verification reminders, login alerts, and system updates.

Each notification document contains the recipient identifier, notification type, associated event reference, creation timestamp, read status, delivery status, and optional expiration date.

Unread notifications remain available until explicitly acknowledged by users. Notification retrieval shall be optimized through indexing on recipient identifiers and read status to ensure efficient dashboard updates.

Historical notifications may be archived or removed automatically after predefined retention periods to reduce unnecessary database growth.

---

# 7.9 Authentication Collection

Authentication-related information requiring independent lifecycle management shall be maintained within dedicated collections separate from permanent user profiles.

Refresh tokens issued during authentication shall be stored securely with associated expiration timestamps and device identifiers. Password reset requests shall maintain temporary reset tokens, expiration periods, verification status, and request timestamps.

Email verification requests similarly store temporary verification tokens allowing users to activate newly created accounts securely. Expired authentication documents shall be removed automatically through MongoDB Time-To-Live (TTL) indexes.

Separating temporary authentication data from permanent user profiles improves security while simplifying automatic cleanup procedures.

---

# 7.10 File Metadata Collection

Uploaded multimedia files shall not be stored directly inside MongoDB due to storage efficiency considerations. Instead, files shall reside within external object storage services while MongoDB maintains descriptive metadata.

Each file document stores the uploader identifier, associated conversation, original filename, storage URL, file size, MIME type, upload timestamp, thumbnail URL (where applicable), encryption status, and scanning results.

This approach significantly reduces database storage requirements while enabling efficient retrieval and management of shared media.

---

# 7.11 Audit Logs Collection

The Audit Logs collection records security-sensitive and administrative events occurring throughout the application lifecycle. Logged activities include successful logins, failed authentication attempts, password modifications, profile updates, account suspensions, administrative actions, system configuration changes, and infrastructure events.

Each log entry contains timestamps, responsible user identifiers, affected resources, originating IP addresses (where applicable), action descriptions, and execution outcomes.

Comprehensive auditing facilitates security investigations, compliance verification, troubleshooting, and operational monitoring while providing administrators with detailed historical records of significant application events.

---

# 7.12 Indexing Strategy

Efficient indexing is essential for maintaining application performance as database size increases. Frequently queried fields shall be indexed to reduce search times and minimize unnecessary collection scans.

Indexes shall be established for usernames, email addresses, conversation identifiers, message timestamps, group memberships, notification recipients, authentication tokens, and file ownership records. Compound indexes may additionally optimize multi-condition queries such as retrieving recent messages belonging to specific conversations.

Careful index selection balances improved query performance against increased storage consumption and write overhead. Database administrators shall periodically evaluate index utilization to identify opportunities for optimization.

---

# 7.13 Data Consistency

Maintaining consistent information throughout a distributed system presents significant challenges. The proposed application adopts a persistence-first strategy whereby every message is permanently stored before publication through Redis Pub/Sub. This sequence guarantees that distributed synchronization never references nonexistent database records.

MongoDB transactions shall be utilized whenever multiple related operations must either complete successfully together or fail entirely. Examples include group creation with initial membership assignment, user registration combined with profile creation, and administrative actions affecting multiple collections.

Duplicate message delivery shall be prevented through globally unique message identifiers and server-side validation mechanisms.

---

# 7.14 Backup and Recovery

Reliable backup procedures are essential for protecting user information against accidental deletion, hardware failures, or software defects. Scheduled database backups shall capture complete snapshots of every collection, including users, conversations, messages, groups, notifications, and configuration data.

Backup files shall be stored securely and periodically verified through restoration testing. Recovery procedures shall minimize downtime while preserving message history and account integrity.

Media files stored externally shall remain independently recoverable through cloud storage providers, ensuring that uploaded content remains available following database restoration.

