# Chapter 11: Use Case Specifications

## 11.1 Introduction

The Use Case Specification defines the interaction between users and the Distributed Web-Based Chat Application. While previous chapters described the architecture, functional requirements, and system behavior, this chapter focuses on how different categories of users interact with the system to accomplish specific objectives. Each use case describes the sequence of events that occur when an actor performs an operation within the application, beginning with the preconditions required before execution and ending with the postconditions achieved after successful completion.

The Distributed Web-Based Chat Application primarily involves three categories of actors: registered users, group administrators, and system administrators. Each actor interacts with the application according to predefined permissions established through the Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) model. The use case specifications describe the normal operational flow, alternative scenarios, exception handling procedures, and interactions among distributed backend services, Redis Pub/Sub, MongoDB, and client applications.

The objective of this chapter is to provide developers, testers, system analysts, and project stakeholders with a complete understanding of user interactions, system responses, and operational workflows throughout the application lifecycle.

---

# 11.2 Actors

The application consists of three primary actors, each having different responsibilities and privileges.

The **Registered User** represents the primary actor within the application. Registered users authenticate themselves using valid credentials and perform activities such as updating profiles, searching users, sending messages, creating conversations, joining groups, uploading files, reacting to messages, and receiving notifications.

The **Group Administrator** is a registered user who possesses additional permissions within group conversations. Group administrators create new groups, invite participants, remove members, modify group settings, assign administrative roles, and manage collaborative communication environments.

The **System Administrator** supervises the overall platform. This actor monitors application health, manages user accounts, investigates reported issues, observes infrastructure performance, reviews application logs, suspends malicious users, and performs system maintenance activities.

---

# 11.3 Overall Use Case Model

The Distributed Web-Based Chat Application supports numerous user interactions that collectively provide a complete real-time communication platform. Every interaction follows predefined workflows ensuring consistency, security, and reliable distributed synchronization.

Major use cases include user registration, user authentication, password recovery, profile management, searching users, initiating private conversations, creating groups, sending messages, uploading files, editing conversations, managing notifications, monitoring online status, administrative moderation, and secure logout.

Each use case operates independently while interacting with common application services including authentication, authorization, database management, Redis synchronization, WebSocket communication, and cloud storage.

---

# 11.4 Use Case 1 – User Registration

## Description

The User Registration use case enables new individuals to create accounts within the application. Registration represents the first interaction between prospective users and the platform. During this process, the system validates user information, securely stores account credentials, creates default profiles, and initiates email verification procedures.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

The individual does not already possess an account associated with the supplied email address or username. Internet connectivity must be available, and backend authentication services must be operational.

## Main Flow

The user accesses the registration page through the web application and enters personal information including full name, username, email address, and password. The frontend performs initial validation before transmitting the request securely to the backend server.

Upon receiving the registration request, the backend validates all submitted information according to predefined business rules. Duplicate usernames or email addresses are rejected immediately. Password complexity requirements are verified, and valid passwords are encrypted using bcrypt before storage.

The application creates a new user document within MongoDB, initializes profile settings, generates an email verification token, and sends a verification email to the registered address. The user receives confirmation that account creation has succeeded while awaiting email verification.

## Alternative Flow

If the submitted email address already exists within the database, the application rejects the registration request and instructs the user to select another email or recover the existing account.

If network connectivity fails during registration, the frontend displays an appropriate error message and allows the user to retry submission.

## Postconditions

A new user account exists within MongoDB, default profile information has been created, and email verification has been initiated.

---

# 11.5 Use Case 2 – User Login

## Description

The User Login use case authenticates existing users before granting access to protected application resources.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

The user must possess a verified account and valid authentication credentials.

## Main Flow

The user navigates to the login interface and submits an email address together with a password. The backend retrieves the corresponding user record from MongoDB and compares the submitted password against the stored bcrypt hash.

If authentication succeeds, the application generates secure JWT access and refresh tokens. The frontend stores authentication information securely and redirects the user to the main application dashboard.

Simultaneously, the backend establishes a WebSocket connection, updates the user's online status within Redis, and synchronizes presence information throughout the distributed system.

## Alternative Flow

If the password is incorrect, the application rejects authentication and increments the failed login counter.

If multiple unsuccessful login attempts occur consecutively, temporary rate limiting or account protection mechanisms may become active.

## Postconditions

The authenticated user gains access to protected application functionality and establishes an active WebSocket session.

---

# 11.6 Use Case 3 – Profile Management

## Description

Profile Management enables authenticated users to modify personal information, privacy preferences, profile photographs, and account settings.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

The user must be authenticated successfully.

## Main Flow

The user accesses the profile management interface and updates one or more profile attributes. The application validates submitted information before updating MongoDB.

If the user uploads a new profile photograph, the image is transferred to cloud storage. After successful upload, the application stores the corresponding image URL within the user's profile document.

Updated profile information is synchronized throughout the distributed infrastructure, ensuring immediate visibility across conversations and contact lists.

## Alternative Flow

Invalid image formats or oversized uploads are rejected before permanent storage.

## Postconditions

The user's profile reflects all successfully validated modifications.

---

# 11.7 Use Case 4 – Search Users

## Description

The Search Users use case allows authenticated users to discover other registered individuals using usernames, display names, or email addresses.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

The user must be logged into the application.

## Main Flow

The user enters search criteria into the search interface. The backend queries indexed MongoDB fields and returns matching user profiles.

Search results display profile information according to privacy settings. Users may immediately initiate conversations or view additional profile information.

## Alternative Flow

If no matching users are found, the application displays an informative message indicating that no results satisfy the search criteria.

## Postconditions

The user obtains relevant search results without modifying application data.

---

# 11.8 Use Case 5 – Start Private Conversation

## Description

Authenticated users initiate private one-to-one conversations with other registered users.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

Both users possess valid registered accounts.

## Main Flow

The initiating user selects another registered user and requests a new conversation. The backend verifies whether an existing conversation already exists between both participants.

If no previous conversation exists, the application creates a new conversation document within MongoDB. The conversation immediately appears within both users' conversation lists.

A persistent WebSocket connection enables subsequent real-time message exchange.

## Alternative Flow

If an existing conversation already exists, the application redirects the user to the previous conversation instead of creating a duplicate.

## Postconditions

A conversation channel exists between both users.

---

# 11.9 Use Case 6 – Send Message

## Description

This use case represents the core functionality of the Distributed Web-Based Chat Application.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

The user is authenticated and currently participating in a conversation.

## Main Flow

The user types a message and presses the Send button. The message is immediately transmitted through the existing WebSocket connection.

The receiving backend server validates the message contents before storing the message permanently within MongoDB.

After successful database insertion, the server publishes a synchronization event through Redis Pub/Sub. Every backend server subscribed to the corresponding channel receives the event.

Servers identify locally connected recipients and instantly deliver the message through active WebSocket connections. Conversation previews, unread message counts, notifications, and timestamps update automatically.

The sender receives confirmation that message delivery has succeeded.

## Alternative Flow

If the recipient is offline, the message remains stored within MongoDB until the recipient reconnects.

Temporary network interruptions trigger automatic reconnection procedures.

## Postconditions

The message is permanently stored and synchronized across the distributed infrastructure.

---

# 11.10 Use Case 7 – Create Group

## Description

Authenticated users create collaborative communication groups involving multiple participants.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

The user is authenticated successfully.

## Main Flow

The creator specifies a group name, optional description, profile image, and initial participants.

The backend creates a new group document within MongoDB while assigning the creator as the initial administrator.

Invitation notifications are generated and delivered to selected participants through Redis Pub/Sub.

The group immediately becomes available for messaging.

## Alternative Flow

If selected users decline invitations, group creation continues with remaining participants.

## Postconditions

A new collaborative communication group exists within the application.

---

# 11.11 Use Case 8 – Upload File

## Description

Users share multimedia content through conversations.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

The user is participating in an active conversation.

## Main Flow

The user selects one or more supported files for upload.

The backend validates file type, file size, and content before uploading the file to cloud storage.

Metadata describing the uploaded file is stored within MongoDB, while the generated storage URL becomes attached to the conversation message.

Redis synchronizes the new message throughout the distributed infrastructure.

Recipients immediately receive download links through WebSocket communication.

## Alternative Flow

Unsupported file types or oversized uploads are rejected before permanent storage.

## Postconditions

The uploaded file becomes accessible to authorized conversation participants.

---

# 11.12 Use Case 9 – Receive Notifications

## Description

The application continuously informs users about important events occurring throughout the platform.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

The user remains authenticated.

## Main Flow

Whenever another participant sends a message, reacts to existing content, mentions the user, or invites the user into a group, the backend generates a notification event.

Redis distributes the notification to all backend servers.

Connected recipients receive immediate WebSocket notifications while offline users retrieve notifications upon subsequent login.

Unread notification counters update automatically.

## Alternative Flow

Temporary communication interruptions delay notification delivery until connectivity resumes.

## Postconditions

Users remain informed about significant application events.

---

# 11.13 Use Case 10 – Logout

## Description

Logout terminates authenticated user sessions securely.

## Primary Actor

Registered User

## Preconditions

The user maintains an active authenticated session.

## Main Flow

The user selects the logout option.

The backend invalidates refresh tokens, removes active session information, updates Redis presence status, closes the WebSocket connection, and records the logout event.

The frontend removes stored authentication tokens before redirecting the user to the login page.

Other users observe updated offline status immediately through distributed synchronization.

## Alternative Flow

Unexpected browser termination triggers automatic session cleanup during connection timeout detection.

## Postconditions

The authenticated session terminates successfully.

---

# 11.14 Use Case 11 – System Administration

## Description

System administrators supervise infrastructure health, application performance, and user management activities.

## Primary Actor

System Administrator

## Preconditions

The administrator possesses valid administrative credentials.

## Main Flow

The administrator authenticates through the administrative interface.

Monitoring dashboards display backend server health, Redis statistics, MongoDB performance, WebSocket activity, application logs, authentication activity, and infrastructure metrics.

Administrators review reported issues, suspend malicious accounts, inspect system logs, and monitor distributed synchronization status.

Maintenance activities occur independently without disrupting ordinary user communication.

## Alternative Flow

If monitoring detects infrastructure failures, alert notifications immediately notify administrators for corrective action.

## Postconditions

Administrative actions are recorded securely within audit logs.

---

# 11.15 Use Case Relationships

Several use cases depend upon the successful completion of earlier interactions. Registration precedes authentication, while authentication is a prerequisite for nearly every protected operation. Messaging depends upon conversation creation, and file uploads extend the messaging workflow. Administrative functions require successful authentication together with elevated authorization privileges. Notification delivery occurs as an extension of numerous independent use cases including messaging, group management, and profile interactions.

The distributed architecture ensures that every use case remains synchronized across backend servers through Redis Pub/Sub while maintaining permanent consistency through MongoDB persistence.

