# Chapter 12: API Specifications and WebSocket Event Specifications

## 12.1 Introduction

The Distributed Web-Based Chat Application provides communication between frontend clients and backend services through two complementary mechanisms: RESTful Application Programming Interfaces (REST APIs) and WebSocket-based real-time communication. REST APIs are responsible for operations that follow a request-response model, such as user authentication, profile management, conversation retrieval, group administration, and file uploads. WebSocket communication provides persistent bidirectional connectivity for real-time messaging, typing indicators, online presence, read receipts, and notification delivery.

Separating these communication mechanisms improves maintainability and performance. Operations requiring immediate synchronization utilize WebSockets, while operations involving resource creation, modification, or retrieval continue using standard HTTP requests. This hybrid architecture ensures efficient network utilization while preserving scalability and compatibility with modern web browsers.

Every API request and WebSocket event follows standardized validation procedures, authentication requirements, authorization checks, error handling policies, and response structures. Consistent interface design simplifies frontend development, reduces implementation errors, and enables future service expansion without disrupting existing functionality.

---

# 12.2 API Architecture

The backend exposes a RESTful API built using Express.js. Every API endpoint is organized according to functional responsibilities including authentication, user management, conversations, messaging, groups, notifications, file management, and administrative operations.

All API requests are transmitted using HTTPS in production environments. Request bodies utilize JSON formatting except file uploads, which use multipart/form-data. Responses follow standardized JSON structures containing status information, response data, metadata, and error descriptions where applicable.

Each backend server exposes identical API endpoints. Incoming requests first reach the Nginx reverse proxy, which forwards them to one of the available backend instances according to the configured load-balancing strategy. Because backend servers remain stateless, any server can process any authenticated request independently.

API versioning shall be implemented using URL prefixes such as **/api/v1/**. Future versions may introduce additional functionality without affecting existing client applications.

---

# 12.3 API Design Principles

The proposed API follows several design principles intended to improve consistency, usability, and maintainability. Every endpoint performs a single well-defined responsibility while maintaining predictable request and response formats. HTTP methods are selected according to resource operations, with GET retrieving information, POST creating resources, PUT updating complete resources, PATCH performing partial updates, and DELETE removing resources.

Resource naming follows plural nouns where appropriate, ensuring that endpoint URLs remain intuitive and self-explanatory. Sensitive operations require authentication before execution, while administrative functions additionally require elevated authorization.

Meaningful HTTP status codes communicate operation outcomes, allowing frontend applications to respond appropriately without relying solely on descriptive text messages.

---

# 12.4 Authentication APIs

Authentication APIs manage account creation, login, logout, password recovery, token refresh, and email verification.

The registration endpoint accepts user information including full name, username, email address, and password. Submitted information undergoes validation before bcrypt password hashing and permanent storage within MongoDB.

The login endpoint authenticates existing users by verifying submitted credentials against stored password hashes. Successful authentication returns JWT access tokens together with refresh tokens enabling secure session continuation.

The logout endpoint invalidates active refresh tokens and terminates authenticated sessions. Password reset endpoints generate secure temporary verification tokens while email verification endpoints activate newly created accounts.

Authentication APIs represent the foundation upon which all protected application functionality depends.

---

# 12.5 User Management APIs

User management endpoints enable authenticated individuals to retrieve profile information, modify personal details, update privacy preferences, upload profile photographs, search registered users, and manage account settings.

Profile retrieval endpoints return publicly visible user information according to configured privacy preferences. Profile modification endpoints validate submitted updates before permanently storing revised information within MongoDB.

Search functionality utilizes indexed database queries to locate users according to usernames, display names, or email addresses while respecting visibility restrictions established by privacy settings.

Administrative endpoints provide additional capabilities including account suspension, activation, and moderation.

---

# 12.6 Conversation APIs

Conversation APIs manage private communication channels between users. Authenticated clients retrieve conversation lists, obtain conversation details, create new conversations, archive existing conversations, and remove inactive conversations through dedicated REST endpoints.

Conversation retrieval supports pagination to improve performance when users possess extensive messaging histories. Summary information includes participant details, latest messages, unread message counts, timestamps, and conversation metadata.

Creation endpoints verify whether identical conversations already exist before creating new database records, preventing duplicate communication channels between identical participants.

Conversation metadata remains synchronized automatically whenever new messages are exchanged through WebSocket communication.

---

# 12.7 Message APIs

Although real-time messaging occurs primarily through WebSockets, REST endpoints support message history retrieval, searching historical conversations, editing existing messages, deleting messages, and retrieving attachment metadata.

Message history endpoints implement pagination, allowing frontend applications to request conversation history incrementally according to scrolling activity. Search functionality enables users to locate historical messages containing specified keywords or phrases.

Message editing preserves modification timestamps while deletion operations update conversation history according to configured retention policies.

These endpoints complement WebSocket communication by providing persistent historical access rather than immediate synchronization.

