# 12.12 Standard API Response Structure

Every successful API response follows a standardized JSON structure containing operation status, descriptive messages, requested data, timestamps, and optional metadata. Standardization simplifies frontend processing because every endpoint produces predictable response formats regardless of functionality.

Error responses similarly provide structured information including error codes, descriptive messages, validation details, and suggested corrective actions where appropriate.

Consistent response formatting reduces implementation complexity while improving interoperability among frontend applications, mobile clients, and future third-party integrations.

---

# 12.13 HTTP Status Codes

The application utilizes standardized HTTP status codes to communicate request outcomes. Successful retrieval operations return status code **200 (OK)**, while resource creation returns **201 (Created)**. Successful operations without response bodies may return **204 (No Content)**.

Client errors include **400 (Bad Request)** for invalid submissions, **401 (Unauthorized)** for missing authentication, **403 (Forbidden)** for insufficient permissions, **404 (Not Found)** when requested resources do not exist, and **409 (Conflict)** for duplicate resources.

Unexpected backend failures return **500 (Internal Server Error)**, while temporary service interruptions may return **503 (Service Unavailable)**.

Meaningful status codes enable frontend applications to implement consistent error handling procedures.

---

# 12.14 WebSocket Architecture

Unlike REST APIs, WebSocket communication maintains persistent bidirectional connections between clients and backend servers. Once authenticated successfully, users establish Socket.IO connections enabling continuous real-time communication.

Backend servers authenticate connection requests before registering active sessions. Connected users remain associated with unique socket identifiers enabling direct message delivery.

Redis Pub/Sub synchronizes events among distributed backend servers, ensuring that users connected to different application instances receive identical real-time updates.

Persistent WebSocket communication significantly reduces latency while eliminating repeated HTTP request overhead.

---

# 12.15 WebSocket Connection Lifecycle

The WebSocket lifecycle begins when authenticated users open the application dashboard. The frontend requests a Socket.IO connection while including the authenticated access token.

Backend servers validate authentication before accepting the connection. Successful connections register active users within Redis and broadcast updated online presence information throughout the distributed infrastructure.

During active sessions, clients exchange messages, typing indicators, read receipts, reactions, and notifications continuously through persistent socket communication.

When users disconnect intentionally or unexpectedly, backend servers remove corresponding presence information and notify remaining participants regarding updated availability.

Automatic reconnection mechanisms restore interrupted sessions whenever network connectivity returns.

---

# 12.16 WebSocket Event Specifications

The application defines several standardized WebSocket events supporting real-time communication.

The **connection** event establishes authenticated communication channels between clients and backend servers. The **disconnect** event terminates active sessions while updating online presence.

The **send_message** event transmits newly composed messages from clients to backend servers. Following database persistence, Redis synchronizes message delivery across distributed infrastructure before recipient notification.

The **receive_message** event delivers synchronized messages to conversation participants immediately following successful processing.

Typing activity utilizes **typing_start** and **typing_stop** events informing recipients about active text composition without permanently storing temporary information.

Read receipts employ **message_read** events updating delivery status whenever recipients view conversation content.

Additional events include **user_online**, **user_offline**, **notification_received**, **message_reaction**, **group_updated**, **member_joined**, **member_removed**, and **conversation_updated**.

These standardized events collectively provide comprehensive real-time functionality.

---

# 12.17 Error Handling

Robust error handling improves application reliability while preventing unexpected failures from disrupting user experience.

Every API request undergoes validation before processing. Invalid requests return descriptive error responses explaining detected problems without exposing sensitive implementation details.

Unexpected backend exceptions generate secure audit log entries while returning generic error messages to clients. Database failures, Redis interruptions, network timeouts, and authentication problems are handled gracefully through standardized recovery procedures.

WebSocket errors similarly notify connected clients whenever synchronization issues occur while automatic reconnection mechanisms attempt recovery whenever possible.

