API endpoints susceptible to abuse, including authentication, registration, password recovery, and search functionality, implement configurable rate limits restricting excessive request generation.

Rate limiting protects infrastructure against brute-force attacks, automated abuse, denial-of-service attempts, and accidental client misconfiguration.

WebSocket events additionally enforce transmission limits preventing malicious users from overwhelming backend servers through excessive event generation.

Appropriate rate limits balance security with legitimate application usage.

---

# 12.19 API Security

Every protected endpoint requires successful JWT authentication before execution. Administrative operations additionally verify role-based authorization.

Input validation, schema verification, parameter sanitization, secure headers, HTTPS encryption, and centralized logging collectively protect API endpoints against unauthorized access and malicious requests.

Sensitive information including passwords, authentication secrets, and internal infrastructure details shall never appear within API responses.

Consistent security enforcement ensures that REST APIs and WebSocket communication remain protected throughout the distributed environment.

